# Where the driver actually lives.
#
# This repository holds the measurement harness and the written record; the
# Honeykrisp changes that produce the speed-up are commits in a Mesa fork. Until
# now that fork was referred to only as "$HOME/Projects/mesa-local", which is
# a path on one machine and means nothing to anybody else. This file is the
# real reference, and it is what ./build-driver.sh and the packaging workflow
# both read.
#
# MESA_COMMIT is a pin, not a suggestion. A package built from "whatever
# local-deploy points at today" cannot say what it was measured with.
#
# The numbers in STATE.md and data/per-fix-results.md were produced at
# 0a5367a9c506c9d7e97e5467556365c3ef54442b. The pin below is one commit later:
# the fixes from an external review (CHECKLIST.md item 45), which touch only
# correctness -- a profiler use-after-free, two holes in the default-OFF
# subqueue overlap, an allocation error path, the cache-key hash -- and none
# of the paths those numbers exercise. CTS results for it are in item 45.

MESA_REPO="https://github.com/aquarat/mesa"
MESA_BRANCH="local-deploy"
MESA_COMMIT="d105715f01c3d82a1e703e8a2d591b017665278a"

# Human-readable version of the tree above. Mesa's own VERSION file says
# 26.3.0-devel; this is what ends up in the RPM version.
MESA_VERSION="26.3.0"

# To move the pin: push to local-deploy, then
#   git ls-remote https://github.com/aquarat/mesa local-deploy
# and paste the sha here in the same commit as whatever documentation change
# the new driver justifies.
